from (values
  (1, $c$Back in the Matrix$c$, $c$Thursday night you turned your phone off.

For three days nobody could reach you. And somewhere in the quiet, something happened in you. Maybe it was big. Maybe it was small enough you're not sure it counts.

It counts.

This morning the phone is back on. By lunch, the weekend might feel like it happened to somebody else. That doesn't mean it wasn't real. It means you're back in the matrix, and the matrix is loud.

This is where you keep it. A few minutes a day.

Every day there's a short reading and audio from me. They're not the same. The reading reminds you. The audio takes it further. Do both.

Then time with Dad. Not a lesson. A conversation.

You didn't do the weekend alone. Don't do this alone either. Join the alumni group below and tell the guys you're in.$c$, $c$Dad gets the first two minutes.$c$, $c$Phone face down. Ask Him: *Father, what don't You want me to lose from this weekend?* Then be quiet and listen. If something comes, put it in your notes. If nothing comes, that's okay. You showed up.$c$, 2, $c$Join the alumni group. Post your name and which camp you went to.$c$, $c$Camp turned the noise off long enough for you to hear. Now you get to choose the quiet. Dad gets the first two minutes.$c$),
  (2, $c$Something Stirred$c$, $c$Remember how Thursday night started? Before anyone said a word, three trailers played. Braveheart. Saving Private Ryan. Open Range.

And something in you sat up.

Most men won't admit that. It feels a little silly to get stirred up by a movie trailer. But it wasn't the movies. It was you. Something in you wants to fight for something. To go somewhere. To matter.

A lot of good men get to where everything looks fine on paper. And underneath sits a question they never say out loud: *Is this it?*

That question isn't ungrateful. It's a clue. Those desires aren't a defect. God put them there. The glory of God is a man fully alive, not a man who has managed his heart into silence.

This week, notice when it stirs again. A song in the truck. Your kid doing something brave. That's your heart, still alive.

And if nothing stirred this weekend, that's okay. Some hearts have been quiet a long time. That's exactly why you're here.$c$, $c$Smoke means fire.$c$, $c$Ask Him: *Father, what did You stir in me this weekend?* Then listen.$c$, 2, $c$Which trailer got you?$c$, $c$What stirred Thursday night wasn't the movies. It was your heart, and it's alive. Smoke means fire.$c$),
  (3, $c$Whip or Well$c$, $c$Thursday night you watched William Wallace ride out in war paint. A few minutes later, Mr. Rogers.

Strange pair. On purpose.

Most of us were handed one of two pictures of a man: hard and loud, or nice and safe. Neither one is the whole thing. Masculinity isn't toxic. And the goal was never to become a nice guy.

Jesus made a whip and cleared the temple. Jesus sat down at a well with a woman everybody else avoided, and was tender with her. Same man. Strong both times.

So how do you know which one a moment needs? There's no rule for that. You pause long enough to get quiet, and you ask Dad.

That's the whole difference. Not knowing more about being a man. Knowing Him well enough to ask.

You'll miss it sometimes. Everybody does. Ask anyway.$c$, $c$Whip or well?$c$, $c$Think of one situation waiting for you today. Ask Him: *Dad, whip or well?* Then listen. If you're not sure what you heard, that's okay. Go in having asked.$c$, 3, $c$When you get caught off guard, where do you default?$c$, $c$Jesus was strong with a whip and strong at a well. You find out which one a moment needs by asking Him.$c$),
  (4, $c$Bored to Death$c$, $c$One week ago tonight, you were sitting in that first session.

You heard three desires named. A battle to fight. An adventure to live. A passion for beauty.

Maybe one of them hit you hard. Maybe you weren't sure any of them fit you anymore. Both are normal.

Remember the pilot in Never Cry Wolf? The real gold isn't in the ground. It's in men sitting in their living rooms, bored to death.

Nobody decides to get bored to death. It happens slowly. You get responsible. You get busy. The things that made you come alive get managed down to a hobby, a screen, a game on Saturday.

These desires aren't a problem to manage. They're how God made you. We'll spend a whole week on each one later. Today, just notice which one still has a pulse.

It's in there. Bored isn't dead.$c$, $c$What's your trumpet?$c$, $c$Ask Him: *Father, what have I let go quiet? What do You want to wake back up?* Whatever comes to mind first, even if it seems small, write it down.$c$, 3, $c$What's your trumpet?$c$, $c$The desires God put in you don't die. They get put in a case. What's your trumpet?$c$),
  (5, $c$How's Your Heart?$c$, $c$Thursday night, right before the ground rules, there was one line: *Your heart matters. It's central to everything God is doing in you.*

Then the counsel. Phone off. No politics. No sports. Drop the religious act. Just be you.

Those weren't really rules. They were a way to protect your heart for three days so you could actually hear it.

You're home now, and everything is fighting for your heart again. The phone. The news. The scores. The pressure to look like you've got it together. None of it is evil. It just keeps you busy enough that you never stop and check.

The heart shows up in Scripture something like 800 times. Don't lose heart. Keep heart. That's not about feelings. It's about the center of you.

The first fight is for your own heart. And you can't fight for something you never check on.$c$, $c$How's your heart?$c$, $c$Let Him ask you: *How's your heart?* Answer honestly. Then listen for what He says back.$c$, 3, $c$One piece of camp counsel you're keeping.$c$, $c$"Good" and "fine" aren't the real answer. Dad wants the real one.$c$),
  (6, $c$Do You Want to Be Healed?$c$, $c$The last thing you saw Thursday night was a man lying by a pool.

He'd been there for years, waiting for a chance that never came. And Jesus walked up and asked him a question that sounds almost rude: *Do you want to be healed?*

The man answered with everything around him. Nobody helps me. Somebody always gets there first.

And Jesus said, *Look at me. That's not what I asked.*

He wasn't asking about who was in the way. He was asking about the man.

He didn't force anything. He invited. He still does.

It's been a week. Picture Him sitting down next to you again, wherever you are right now. Same question.

You don't have to have the right answer. You don't have to be finished. You don't even have to be sure what you'd want healed. You just have to let Him ask.$c$, $c$Look at me.$c$, $c$Picture Jesus sitting next to you. Let Him ask: *Do you want to be healed?* Answer Him honestly, then listen. Write down what comes. If what comes is quiet, that's okay too.$c$, 5, $c$One line about your first week home.$c$, $c$Jesus asks about you, not about who's in your way. Look at me.$c$),
  (7, $c$One Week Home$c$, $c$You made it a week.

Maybe you did every day. Maybe you missed a few. Maybe the weekend already feels far away. All of that is normal.

Here's what isn't true: that if the feeling faded, it wasn't real. Feelings fade. What God did doesn't.

Today, rest. Nothing new. Your notes from this week are right below. Read back through what you wrote. You might be surprised.

Then think of one man from your weekend. A guy from your table, your fire, your group. Send him a text. It doesn't have to be deep. *Hey, thinking about you. How's your heart?*

Tomorrow starts something new. That's all I'll say about it.

You showed up this week. That counts.$c$, $c$Faded isn't fake.$c$, $c$Ask Him: *What did You do this week that I missed?*$c$, 3, $c$Text one guy from your weekend, then post that you did.$c$, $c$Feelings fade. What God did doesn't. Faded isn't fake.$c$),
  (8, $c$You Are Here$c$, $c$Ever stood in front of one of those big maps at the mall? You look for one thing first. The red dot. *You are here.*

Until you find it, the rest of the map is useless.

That's what Friday morning at camp was. The Larger Story. A map, and a red dot.

Most of us walked into life like walking into a movie twenty minutes late. Stuff is blowing up. Nobody tells you the plot. You just try to keep up. And some Wednesdays, life doesn't feel like a story at all. It feels like a mugging.

Here's what that session was saying: your life is not random. There is a larger story, and you're in it. This week we walk back through it, one act at a time.

You don't have to remember all of it. You just need to find the red dot.

You are here.$c$, $c$You are here.$c$, $c$Ask Him: *Father, where am I in Your story right now?* Then listen. If nothing's clear yet, that's okay. You just started reading the map.$c$, 3, $c$Where does life feel like you walked in twenty minutes late?$c$, $c$Life isn't random. You walked in twenty minutes late, and nobody gave you the map. This week you found the red dot.$c$),
  (9, $c$Control, Crazy, Check Out$c$, $c$Remember the frogs? In the middle of the night, frogs start falling out of the sky and smashing into a windshield. Nobody knows why.

That's how life can feel. And when it does, men reach for one of three moves.

Control. Grip tighter. Plan harder. Make everybody fall in line.

Crazy. Blow up. Worry, rage, spin out.

Check out. Screens. Work. A beer. Whatever makes it go quiet.

Most of us have a favorite. Some of us use all three before lunch.

Hear this the way it was said at camp: these aren't a diagnosis. You're not broken for doing them. They're what a man does when he's lost the map. They kept you going.

But they cost you. Control suffocates the people closest to you. Crazy scares them. Checking out leaves them alone.

You're not defective. You're disoriented. And that can change.$c$, $c$Not defective. Disoriented.$c$, $c$Ask Him: *Father, which one do I reach for?* Then: *What do You want to give me instead?* Listen. No beating yourself up.$c$, 3, $c$Control, crazy, or check out: which is your go-to?$c$, $c$When life makes no sense, men grab, blow up, or disappear. That's not who you are. Not defective. Disoriented.$c$),
  (10, $c$Made for Fellowship$c$, $c$Before there was a war, before there was a world, there was fellowship.

That's where the story starts. Not Genesis 1. Before it. *In the beginning was the Word.* Father, Son, and Spirit, together. A heroic fellowship.

Remember the opening of The Last of the Mohicans? No words. A father and his two sons running through the forest together. Fast. Free. Together.

That's the picture. That's Act One.

And you were made in that image. That's why something in you lights up when you're with a few good men doing something hard together. It's why you still want your Father's attention, even as a grown man. That hunger isn't weakness. It's the image of God in you.

You weren't made to run alone. You were made to run with Him. And with brothers.

The story started good. Really good. Hang on to that. You'll need it tomorrow.$c$, $c$Made for fellowship.$c$, $c$Don't ask Him for anything today. Just say: *I'm here, Dad.* And be with Him. If that feels strange, that's okay. Stay anyway.$c$, 3, $c$Tag one man you'd want to run with.$c$, $c$The story doesn't start with a problem. It starts with fellowship, and you were made in that image.$c$),
  (11, $c$Born Into a War$c$, $c$Ever notice every great story has a villain?

There's a reason. Yours does too.

Act Two is where the story turns. God made angels, and they're not chubby babies with harps. They're fierce. Think Navy SEALs. And the most beautiful of them all, Lucifer, decided he didn't want to serve. He wanted the throne. He rebelled and took a third of the angels with him.

God threw him out of heaven. But He didn't take him out of the story.

So here's the truth most of us never heard growing up: you were born into a world at war.

That's not meant to scare you. It's meant to make sense of things. Why life is this hard. Why it feels like something's against you. Why the good things you go after always seem to cost something.

It's not because you're cursed. It's because you matter.$c$, $c$Every story has a villain.$c$, $c$Ask Him: *Father, what do You see in me that's worth fighting over?* Then listen.$c$, 3, $c$One place you're pushing toward life right now.$c$, $c$Every story has a villain because yours does. Hardship doesn't mean you're cursed. It means you matter.$c$),
  (12, $c$He Hit the Beach$c$, $c$Act Three starts in a garden.

Everything is good. And the enemy shows up with one message. It wasn't really about fruit. It was this: *You can't trust the heart of God.*

Adam bought it. Most of us still do. It's the lie under almost every other lie.

But God didn't leave. He set the stage for a rescue. And then He came Himself.

Christmas wasn't a sweet little manger scene. It was an invasion. Remember Omaha Beach? Men running into gunfire for people they'd never met. That's closer to what Jesus did. He landed in enemy territory to set captives free. That was His mission, right out of Isaiah 61: heal the brokenhearted, free the prisoners.

They killed Him. And three days later, He walked out of the grave. The only one ever to come back from that fight alive.

He didn't come to make you nicer. He came to get you.$c$, $c$He hit the beach for me.$c$, $c$Ask Him honestly: *Father, where don't I trust Your heart?* Then listen. He's not offended by the question.$c$, 3, $c$Where have you seen Him come after you?$c$, $c$The oldest lie is that you can't trust God's heart. His answer wasn't an argument. It was an invasion.$c$),
  (13, $c$Not Yet$c$, $c$Remember the end of Gladiator? Maximus is going home to his family. And the line: *"I will see you again. But not yet."*

That's Act Four.

Most of us were sold a version of heaven that sounds like an endless church service. No wonder nobody's in a hurry. That's not the story.

The story ends with restoration. God says, *I make all things new.* Not all new things. All things new. This world, put right. What was lost, given back. Everything that was ever good, healed and whole and forever.

We've been ripped off of Act Four. And a man who doesn't know how the story ends will settle for whatever he can grab now.

But we're not there yet. We're still in Act Three. That's why it's still hard.

Remember what Sam told Frodo? There's some good in this world, and it's worth fighting for.

Not yet. But it's coming.$c$, $c$Not yet.$c$, $c$Ask Him: *Father, what are You going to make new?* Then listen, and write down what comes. Let yourself hope a little.$c$, 5, $c$What are you looking forward to seeing made new?$c$, $c$The story ends with all things made new. We're not there yet. But it's coming, and it's worth fighting for.$c$),
  (14, $c$Live Awake$c$, $c$Two weeks home.

This week you got the map. Fellowship before the war. A villain who comes after you because you matter. A God who hit the beach to get you back. An ending worth waiting for.

And the red dot. You are here. Act Three. In the middle of the fight, with the ending still coming.

Most men sleepwalk through that story. They just try to get through the day. The invitation is to live awake. To see your Tuesday as part of something bigger. Your marriage. Your work. Your kids. The fight you're in.

Your notes from this week are right below. Read back through them. You might see more of the story than you thought.

Today, rest.

And if one question is sitting with you, let it sit: *If this is the story, why am I not living like a free man?*

Tomorrow we start there.$c$, $c$Live awake.$c$, $c$Ask Him: *Father, what did You show me this week?* Then just be with Him.$c$, 3, $c$One line about your second week home.$c$, $c$You're in Act Three. You don't need the whole plot. Just keep finding the red dot.$c$)
) as v(sort_index, title, body_text, carry_line, with_dad_prompt, with_dad_minutes, share_prompt, recap_line)
